(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 58-year-old man is admitted with coronavirus disease 2019. His oxygen saturation is 90% on room air, and he needs supplemental oxygen. Which medication is the core treatment?","choices":["Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir","Azithromycin","Dexamethasone","Molnupiravir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized adults with hypoxemia receive dexamethasone, usually for up to 10 days or until discharge, with remdesivir considered by timing and respiratory support. Oxygenation and severity, not simply a positive test or elapsed time, decide the pathway, and his saturation of 90% puts him on it. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 121, 122, 126"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man on home oxygen for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease arrives with burns to his lips and nose after lighting a cigarette while wearing his cannula. Which hazard does this show?","choices":["Hypercapnia","Fire","Mucosal irritation","Vasoconstriction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A man on home oxygen lights a cigarette while wearing his cannula and burns his lips and nose. Oxygen is highly flammable, and fire is a listed hazard of oxygen therapy, which is why no smoking is taught for every home oxygen user. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 7, 9"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has an exacerbation with marked breathlessness. Before attributing it to the exacerbation, his clinician wants to exclude acute heart failure. Which test is most useful for that purpose?","choices":["Spirometry with a bronchodilator","B-type natriuretic peptide","6-minute walk test","Alpha-1 antitrypsin level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Exacerbation evaluation includes oxygen saturation, a blood gas, a chest x-ray, an electrocardiogram, and B-type natriuretic peptide or troponin. B-type natriuretic peptide is the test for suspected associated heart failure, one of the diagnoses to exclude. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 56"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease who smokes is asked about his smoking and is clearly advised to quit, with a link to his diabetes and heart risk. Using the Five A's approach, which step should come next?","choices":["Arrange a follow-up visit","Assess his readiness to change","Ask about his smoking again","Assist with a quit plan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After Ask and Advise, the Five A's continue with Assess, evaluating the person's readiness and willingness to change, then Assist with actionable steps, and Arrange follow-up. Readiness shapes how the clinician helps. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which presentation separates pneumonia from acute bronchitis?","choices":["Higher fever with tachypnea","Central substernal wheeze","Low-grade fever with body aches","Cough with colored sputum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Apart from walking pneumonia, pneumonia presents more severely than bronchitis: higher fevers and chills, pleuritic or atypical chest pain, a productive cough and shortness of breath with tachypnea. Bronchitis tends to bring a low-grade fever. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 16"},{"q":"A 17-year-old runner with exercise-induced bronchospasm uses albuterol before every practice, yet still has frequent symptoms during training. Which change is most likely to prevent his episodes?","choices":["Stopping exercise permanently","Saving albuterol for after symptoms start","Cromolyn sodium taken during symptoms","Regular inhaled corticosteroid use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exercise-induced bronchospasm is treated with a short-acting beta-2 agonist before exercise but is best prevented with regular inhaled corticosteroid use. His frequent symptoms despite pre-exercise albuterol call for the regular controller. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 66, 96"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is starting pulmonary rehabilitation and asks what it will involve. Which description is accurate?","choices":["A home program done alone","A surgical procedure on the lungs","Weekly antibiotics to prevent infection","Breathing and secretion-clearing training"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary rehabilitation covers clearing secretions, breathing techniques, strengthening exercises, energy conservation and avoiding fatigue, and patient education. It cannot be done alone; it needs a healthcare professional or interdisciplinary team. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which two airways are listed as artificial airways, as opposed to tracheal airways?","choices":["Nasal cannula and simple mask","Endotracheal tube and tracheostomy","Venturi mask and trach collar","Oropharyngeal and nasopharyngeal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The oropharyngeal airway and the nasopharyngeal airway are the two listed as artificial airways. The tracheal airways, the endotracheal tube and tracheostomy, are listed separately. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman who moved to a high-altitude town months ago has a chronic respiratory alkalosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 27 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which serum electrolyte pattern accompanies her renal compensation?","choices":["Low chloride and high bicarbonate","Normal chloride with a high anion gap","High potassium and low chloride","High chloride and low potassium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In respiratory alkalosis the kidneys excrete bicarbonate and retain chloride, giving a reduced bicarbonate, hyperchloremia and hypokalemia (cells also secrete hydrogen ions in exchange for potassium). Her bicarbonate of 19 shows that compensation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has an exacerbation. After treatment in the office his breathing is somewhat better, but he has new swelling of both legs, he lives alone, and he has no one to help at home. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Hospital admission","Pulmonary rehabilitation referral","Intensive care admission","Outpatient oral treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Admission criteria include new physical signs such as edema, significant comorbidities with an exacerbation, age, and lack of home or outpatient resources. His new edema, advanced age and lack of support at home meet several of them. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman who works in a furniture-finishing plant has had cough and wheezing for 6 months. She notes the symptoms ease on weekends and nearly vanish during a 2-week vacation, then return within days of going back to work. She has no history of allergies. Which diagnosis best explains her pattern?","choices":["Catamenial asthma","Extrinsic allergic asthma","Occupational asthma","Exercise-induced bronchospasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms that improve on weekends and vacations and return at work point to occupational asthma from a workplace sensitizer, here chemicals in furniture finishing. Occupational sensitization is independent of atopy, so her lack of allergies does not argue against it. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 36"},{"q":"What is the treatment of metabolic acidosis, whether the anion gap is normal or elevated?","choices":["Give isotonic saline for every case","Treat the underlying disorder","Stop the diuretics","Breathe into a paper bag"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For both increased and normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, the treatment is to treat the underlying disorder. The compensatory response is also assessed, using the Winter formula. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 35, 44, 56, 57"},{"q":"What is the preferred treatment for psittacosis in adults?","choices":["Amoxicillin","A macrolide","Doxycycline","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Doxycycline is preferred in adults, started promptly when psittacosis is strongly suspected; improvement often begins within 48 to 72 hours. If tetracyclines are unsuitable, a macrolide is discussed with infectious disease, though macrolide failures have occurred. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease says that, walking on level ground, he has to stop for breath after about 100 meters or after a few minutes. Which modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Grade 3 is stopping for breath after walking about 100 meters or after a few minutes on the level. A grade of 2 or more counts as more symptoms, which places him in group B or E depending on exacerbation risk. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia is newly disoriented to place and time. His blood urea nitrogen is 31 mg/dL, respiratory rate 32/min and blood pressure 86/52 mm Hg. What is his CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score?","choices":["2","3","4","5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Confusion 1 (new disorientation) + urea 1 (31 is above 20) + respiratory rate 1 (32 is 30 or more) + blood pressure 1 (systolic below 90) + age 0 (58) = 4. That is high severity, with a risk of death of 15 to 40%: hospital, urgent senior review and a decision about critical care, especially at 4 or 5. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with severe persistent asthma has needed 4 courses of oral corticosteroids this year despite confirmed adherence and good technique with her inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting beta-2 agonist. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Add theophylline","Refer to a lung or allergy specialist","Start allergen immunotherapy at home","Stop the long-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With adherence and technique confirmed, her repeated steroid-requiring exacerbations make her asthma refractory. Monoclonal antibody agents are used by pulmonology and allergy specialists and reduced the need for corticosteroids in trials, so referral to a specialist fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 84, 91"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has glaucoma treated with an oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, type 2 diabetes treated with metformin, and daily aspirin use for headaches. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 115 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which of her medicines best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Metformin","Aspirin","A respiratory cause, not a medicine","The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and low bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis; the gap is 140 - (115 + 15) = 10, within 9 to 15, with a high chloride: a normal-gap, hyperchloremic acidosis. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are on the normal-gap list (loss of bicarbonate). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 47, 52, 55, 56, 58"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has asthma symptoms only 1 day a week and uses albuterol 1 day a week, but she wakes at night with cough twice a week. She has no activity limitation, and her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 86% predicted. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severity is assigned to the most severe category in which any feature occurs. Her daytime symptoms and lung function are intermittent-level, but night awakenings more than once a week (twice weekly) are a moderate persistent feature, so she is moderate persistent. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and few symptoms asks to be started on an inhaled corticosteroid by itself because a friend with asthma uses one. Which regimen should be avoided in him?","choices":["A yearly influenza vaccine","An inhaled corticosteroid alone","Smoking cessation with counseling","A single short-acting bronchodilator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In this disease, inhaled corticosteroids must be combined with a long-acting beta agonist, and then with a long-acting antimuscarinic as triple therapy, and cannot be used on their own. They also raise pneumonia risk. Alone, they are an asthma treatment. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with myasthenia gravis has worsening weakness and shallow, labored breathing over a day. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.27 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 57 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Acute respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.27 is acidemia, and the carbon dioxide of 57 is high and matches it (respiratory). Bicarbonate rose only about 1 per 10 mm Hg (17 above 40 gives about 26), so it is acute. Myasthenia gravis is a listed neuromuscular cause of acute respiratory acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 22, 26, 59"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with chronic kidney failure who has missed several dialysis sessions is nauseated and fatigued. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.26 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 29 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 13 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 100 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory alkalosis","Elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.26 is acidemia, and the bicarbonate of 13 is low and matches it: metabolic. Gap = 138 - (100 + 13) = 25, above 9 to 15, so it is elevated; uremia is an elevated-gap cause. The carbon dioxide of 29 is compensation (Winter 1.5 x 13 + 8 = 27.5, plus or minus 2). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman meets the criteria for macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease, without cavitation or severe disease. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Inhaled liposomal amikacin as first therapy","Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol","Azithromycin, ethambutol and a rifamycin","Azithromycin alone until cultures clear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex pulmonary disease is treated with azithromycin, ethambutol and a rifamycin, under specialist direction, continued for at least 12 months after culture conversion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with asthma, already on an inhaled corticosteroid and a long-acting beta-2 agonist, has heart disease and worries about side effects. Her clinician is considering adding tiotropium. Which property of tiotropium is reassuring for her?","choices":["Fast onset that suits rescue use","Little systemic absorption","A strong anti-inflammatory effect","Greater effect than beta-2 agonists"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tiotropium, an anticholinergic, has little or no systemic absorption and therefore few side effects. It inhibits only the cholinergic reflex part of bronchoconstriction and mucus secretion, and it is less effective than beta-2 agonists with a slow onset. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension treated with a thiazide diuretic is light-headed on standing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 46 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.2 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is low. Along with isotonic saline, which medication change is indicated?","choices":["Start a mineralocorticoid","Start a calcium carbonate antacid","Stop the thiazide diuretic","Add a second diuretic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low urine chloride and orthostatic symptoms mark a saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, and diuretic therapy is a listed cause. Treatment is isotonic saline for the volume deficit and discontinuing the diuretic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease had 3 moderate exacerbations in the past year. Her blood eosinophil count is 110. She is starting maintenance therapy. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Triple inhaled therapy","Daily oral corticosteroids","A single bronchodilator","Dual long-acting bronchodilators"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Three moderate exacerbations make her high risk, group E, which starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic. Her eosinophil count of 110 is below 300, so an inhaled corticosteroid, with its pneumonia risk, is not added. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a severe asthma attack is tiring, and her carbon dioxide has risen over an hour to 60 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). Using an acute bicarbonate rise of about 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide, from midpoints of 40 and 24, what bicarbonate is expected?","choices":["24 mEq/L","26 mEq/L","31 mEq/L","34 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbon dioxide rose 20 (40 to 60); acutely bicarbonate rises about 1 per 10, so 24 + 2 = 26 mEq/L. Asthma is a listed cause of acute respiratory acidosis, and a near-normal bicarbonate marks an acute process. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has mild hypoxia, saturation 87%, during a cold. A student suggests a non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min \"to be safe.\" Why should that be avoided?","choices":["It is meant for severe hypoxia","It cannot raise his saturation","It is a fire hazard in hospitals","It causes vasoconstriction in the lungs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His hypoxia is mild at 87%, and a non-rebreather mask is used in severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well, delivering 60-100% at 10-15 L/min. For mild hypoxia the nasal cannula is the most commonly used device, so the non-rebreather overshoots his need. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 11, 15"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with severe obesity and a high fever from pneumonia has a carbon dioxide of 62 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). What usually causes a raised carbon dioxide like his?","choices":["Under-excretion from hypoventilation","Hyperventilation","Excess production from the fever","Excess bicarbonate retained by the kidneys"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypercapnia is usually the result of under-excretion (hypoventilation), not excess production. At steady state carbon dioxide production matches excretion, so a high level means ventilation is not keeping up. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man is about to start a tumor necrosis factor inhibitor. He has no symptoms of tuberculosis and has never been tested for it. Which step regarding tuberculosis is most appropriate before he starts?","choices":["Targeted testing for tuberculosis infection","Empiric latent-infection treatment","Three sputum specimens for culture","No testing because he has no symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tumor necrosis factor inhibitors raise the risk of progression after tuberculosis infection, and testing is targeted at people at increased risk. A skin test or interferon-gamma release assay detects the immune response to infection; a positive result then needs clinical evaluation and chest imaging. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 19"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure is on oxygen while his fluid overload is treated. Over two days his breathing eases, his saturation stays at target, and his vital signs settle. Which ongoing step is part of evaluating his oxygen therapy?","choices":["Stop checking his saturation","Keep monitoring for complications","Start home oxygen qualification today","Raise his flow to secure the gains"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Evaluating response ends with vital signs and further monitoring for complications. His breathing has eased and his saturation holds, but he remains on oxygen, so the hazards of oxygen therapy still need watching. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which toxic or pharmacologic agent causes respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Licorice","Recreational opioids","Intravenous sedation","Salicylates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The toxic and pharmacologic causes of respiratory alkalosis are salicylates, caffeine, nicotine, catecholamines and thyroxine. Each drives hyperventilation, which lowers carbon dioxide. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Between exacerbations, the physical examination of a patient with asthma is often what?","choices":["Marked by a barrel chest","Marked by digital clubbing","Marked by fixed wheezing","Normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The examination may be normal between exacerbations, which is why an objective lung function measurement is needed for the diagnosis. Extrinsic asthma may still show nasal mucosal swelling, increased secretions, eczema or nasal polyps. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man with spirometry-confirmed asthma notices flares in spring and whenever he visits a friend with dogs. He wants to know exactly what he is reacting to. Which test identifies his triggers?","choices":["Repeat spirometry with a bronchodilator","Skin or blood allergy testing","Methacholine challenge","Fractional exhaled nitric oxide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Allergy testing, by skin or blood test, is useful to identify allergen triggers. His seasonal and dog-related flares suggest pollen and pet dander, the inhaled allergens that activate mast cells, and testing guides which ones to avoid. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 38, 59"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with severe community-acquired pneumonia in the intensive care unit had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus isolated from her sputum last year. She has been started on ceftriaxone plus azithromycin. Which agent should be added?","choices":["Clindamycin","Moxifloxacin","Vancomycin","Ertapenem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage is added only for validated risks, and a prior respiratory isolate is one. The agents named for it are vancomycin or linezolid, added to the beta-lactam combination; cultures are obtained and therapy de-escalated as results return. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 40, 44"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with cirrhosis and an albumin of 1.9 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.0 g/dL) has a metabolic acidosis, a chloride of 110 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L) and an anion gap of 11 (normal 9 to 15). Which finding most limits how much the normal gap can reassure you?","choices":["Her low serum albumin","Her metabolic acidosis itself","A low unmeasured cation level","Her high chloride"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Albumin accounts for most of the normal anion gap, so hypoalbuminemia lowers the gap and can hide added unmeasured anions. A normal gap does not exclude a high-gap acidosis and should not override clinical concern. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has had a cough for 6 days with yellow sputum. His temperature is 101.8°F and his respiratory rate 24/min, and there are crackles with dullness to percussion over the left lower lobe. He asks for an antibiotic because his sputum is yellow. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Supportive care for bronchitis","An antibiotic for the yellow sputum","A sputum culture before deciding","A chest radiograph to confirm pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever, a raised respiratory rate and focal crackles with dullness are the findings that raise concern for pneumonia rather than uncomplicated bronchitis, and suspected pneumonia is confirmed with a chest radiograph. The yellow sputum is not what changes the plan: the fever and focal findings are. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 18, 29"},{"q":"Alongside clinical judgment, which tool is preferred to support the admission decision in community-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["Pneumonia Severity Index","Chest radiograph extent","White blood cell count","Sputum culture result"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Pneumonia Severity Index plus clinical judgment is preferred for admission decisions; the five-item confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure and age score complements it but does not replace clinical judgment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia is intubated for respiratory failure and admitted to the intensive care unit. He has no validated risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Ceftriaxone as a single drug","Vancomycin plus cefepime","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Levofloxacin monotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mechanical ventilation makes this severe community-acquired pneumonia, treated with a beta-lactam plus azithromycin or a respiratory fluoroquinolone. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas coverage is added only for validated risks, which he lacks; cultures are obtained and therapy de-escalated later. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 44, 45"},{"q":"When should palliative care be integrated for older adults with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["In the final weeks of life","After every medicine has failed","Early, with caregivers involved","When the patient asks for it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For older adults palliative care is integrated early, with caregiver involvement and advance directives. For working-age adults it is introduced as the disease advances. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which tests are requested on the sputum specimens in suspected pulmonary tuberculosis?","choices":["Serum antigen and antibody","Tuberculin skin test reading","Acid-fast smear and culture","Interferon-gamma release assay"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Each specimen is sent for an acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, with a rapid molecular test on at least one respiratory specimen and molecular and culture-based drug-susceptibility testing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man who aspirated gastric contents during a witnessed seizure was managed with oxygen and airway support and no antibiotic. Three days later he is worse, with a new fever, purulent sputum and a persistent infiltrate. Which complication should be suspected?","choices":["Resolving chemical injury","Asthma exacerbation","Viral acute bronchitis","Secondary bacterial infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pneumonitis is managed with support, and persistence or worsening raises concern for secondary infection, a complication or another diagnosis. New fever, purulent sputum and a persistent infiltrate after 3 days point to a secondary bacterial infection, which now needs the applicable pneumonia regimen. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman's roommate was diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis 10 weeks ago, and they have lived apart since. She has no symptoms. Her tuberculin skin test, read at 72 hours, shows 7 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Positive; any measurable induration counts","Positive; recent close contacts use 5 mm","Negative; her cut-off is 10 mm","Negative; her cut-off is 15 mm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A recent close contact of infectious tuberculosis is in the 5 mm group, with human immunodeficiency virus infection, transplant, major immunosuppression and imaging suggesting prior tuberculosis. 7 mm is at least 5 mm, so the test is positive and she needs evaluation for active disease. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 22, 41"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with no medical conditions has never received a pneumococcal vaccine. Which pneumococcal vaccination is appropriate for him?","choices":["One 20-valent conjugate vaccine dose","Yearly pneumococcal vaccination","Wait until he turns 65","A 15-valent conjugate vaccine alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All adults aged 50 or older who have never had a pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, or whose history is unknown, are vaccinated. One dose of the 20-valent or 21-valent conjugate vaccine completes the series; a 15-valent conjugate vaccine is usually followed by the 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 124"},{"q":"Which statement about sputum production in this disease is accurate?","choices":["Its absence rules out the disease","It is present in every patient","It is typical of pure emphysema","It reflects chronic bronchitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients do not always produce sputum: it is seen with chronic bronchitis or a combined presentation and is typically not prevalent in emphysema-only cases. Its absence does not exclude the disease. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why is an interferon-gamma release assay preferred over the tuberculin skin test for most BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin)-vaccinated patients?","choices":["The vaccine makes skin tests falsely negative","It separates latent from active disease","Its cut-offs are adjusted for vaccine history","It is not made positive by the vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccination does not cause a positive interferon-gamma release assay, which measures immune responses to tuberculosis-specific antigens, whereas the vaccine can cause a false-positive skin test. The assay is therefore preferred for most vaccinated patients. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 24, 21, 19"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man with mild persistent asthma well controlled on an inhaled corticosteroid dislikes inhalers and asks to switch to montelukast tablets alone. Which statement should guide the counseling?","choices":["It works as well as his inhaled steroid","It is less effective than his inhaled steroid","It is a rescue medicine for attacks","It is a long-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Montelukast is a leukotriene modifier with modest clinical benefit, useful as add-on therapy, and less effective than inhaled corticosteroids as monotherapy. His inhaled steroid is the preferred first-line treatment for persistent asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 74, 83"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man who moved 6 months ago from an area with high tuberculosis incidence has 2 months of pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, fever and weight loss. Imaging shows a unilateral pleural effusion with no lung infiltrate, and his interferon-gamma release assay is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial pneumonia","Latent tuberculosis infection","Spinal tuberculosis","Pleural tuberculosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tuberculosis can involve almost any organ; pleural disease presents with effusion, pleuritic pain or dyspnea, and fever and weight loss fit tuberculosis. His residence in a high-incidence area is an exposure risk and the positive assay shows infection; organ-specific specimens may be needed. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 14"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a resting oxygen saturation of 91% on room air, and she stays at 91% during a walking test. She asks for home oxygen. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["She needs oxygen over 15 hours a day","She needs oxygen during exertion","No long-term oxygen is indicated","She needs a sleep study first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A resting saturation of 89% to 93% is moderate resting desaturation, with no indication for long-term oxygen, and her saturation stays at 91% with exertion. Oxygen is indicated with severe hypoxemia, an arterial oxygen pressure under 55 mmHg or saturation of 88% or less. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman had asthma as a young child, then had no symptoms throughout adolescence. Over the past 2 months her episodic wheezing and night cough have returned. Which statement best explains her course?","choices":["Asthma can return in adulthood","Her first diagnosis must have been wrong","Asthma that remits in adolescence stays gone","Wheeze returning at 22 means cardiac asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma presents at any age, with a peak at 3 years; it may become asymptomatic during adolescence and may return in adulthood. Her childhood asthma, quiet teenage years and returning symptoms follow that pattern. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and an echocardiogram showing pulmonary hypertension has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. Which statement about home oxygen applies to her?","choices":["She qualifies in Group II","She does not qualify at 89%","She qualifies in Group I","She qualifies for sleep oxygen alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her resting saturation of 89% is above the Group I cutoff of 88% or less, so on its own it would not qualify. The echocardiogram showing pulmonary hypertension adds the comorbidity that makes a saturation of 89% qualify, which is Group II (recertified at 3 months). — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What percentage of patients with asthma become worse with aspirin?","choices":["1 to 5%","10 to 15%","25 to 30%","50 to 60%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. About 1 to 5% of asthmatics become worse with aspirin, through a genetic predisposition that increases inflammatory mediators. Aspirin is among the medication triggers to avoid. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetes and an acute myocardial infarction has an oxygen saturation of 87% on room air. Which is the right approach to oxygen?","choices":["Wait for an arterial blood gas first","Withhold oxygen because of vasoconstriction","Give oxygen, since saturation is below 91%","Withhold oxygen because of her diabetes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her saturation of 87% is below the 91% cutoff. Oxygen is held back in a myocardial infarction because of vasoconstriction, but only while the saturation is 91% or higher. She is hypoxic, so the benefit of correcting hypoxia wins and she should receive oxygen. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman is admitted for repair of a hip fracture. On hospital day 4 she develops fever, purulent sputum and a new infiltrate that was not on her admission chest radiograph. She has not been intubated. How should this pneumonia be classified?","choices":["Hospital-acquired pneumonia","Aspiration pneumonitis","Ventilator-associated pneumonia","Community-acquired pneumonia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hospital-acquired pneumonia develops 48 hours or more after admission and was not incubating on admission. Her new infiltrate appeared on day 4 after a clear admission film, and she was never intubated, so the ventilator label does not apply. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man is admitted with cough, night sweats and weight loss, and his sputum smear shows acid-fast bacilli. Infectious pulmonary tuberculosis is presumed. Where should he be placed?","choices":["Airborne infection isolation room","Contact precautions with gown and gloves","Hallway bed until cultures return","Shared ward with standard precautions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Presumed infectious pulmonary tuberculosis calls for an airborne infection isolation room when indicated, with fit-tested N95 or higher-level respiratory protection for staff, because pulmonary disease releases infectious aerosols during coughing, speaking or singing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 33"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has had a sudden fever, myalgias, a frontal headache and a dry cough for one day, at the peak of a busy influenza season. A rapid influenza antigen test is negative. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["It points to a bacterial cause","It does not exclude influenza","It rules out influenza","She is no longer contagious"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rapid antigen tests have limited sensitivity, and a negative result does not exclude influenza, especially during high community activity. Her sudden typical illness at the peak of the season keeps influenza likely; molecular testing confirms it when the result affects management. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with no immune problems developed fever, chills, a nonproductive cough and chest pain after clearing soil under a bird roost, and her urine Histoplasma antigen is positive. Five weeks later she is no better: her cough and fatigue are worsening, but she is not hypoxemic and is managing at home. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Continued observation","Amphotericin-based induction","Fluconazole for 6 to 12 months","Oral itraconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Histoplasmosis is treated by severity and host risk. Her illness is prolonged and progressive but not severe, so itraconazole is considered, with monitoring of interactions and drug levels; observation fits mild acute disease in an immunocompetent adult, and amphotericin is for severe disease. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 76, 77, 78, 123"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman with asthma has a personal best peak flow of 450 L/min. This morning she has cough and chest tightness, and her peak flow is 300 L/min (67% of her best). According to her written asthma action plan, which zone is she in?","choices":["Not classifiable without spirometry","Red zone","Green zone","Yellow zone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her reading of 300 of a best 450 is 67%, inside the yellow zone of 50% to 79% of personal best, where asthma is getting worse. The plan adds quick-relief medicine (a short-acting beta-2 agonist) while she keeps taking her green-zone controller. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with a bicarbonate of 9 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) from ethylene glycol ingestion has a carbon dioxide of 28 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, what does her carbon dioxide show?","choices":["Appropriate compensation","Renal compensation for the acidosis","An added respiratory acidosis","An added respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 9 + 8 = 21.5, plus or minus 2, so about 19.5 to 23.5. Her 28 is higher than expected, so she has a metabolic acidosis together with a respiratory acidosis, which can be a sign of impending respiratory failure. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 22, 58, 59"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with human immunodeficiency virus infection starts trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for Pneumocystis pneumonia. Her arterial oxygen pressure on room air is 61 mm Hg. Which therapy should be added?","choices":["Intravenous pentamidine","A systemic corticosteroid","No adjunct is needed","An inhaled corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adjunctive corticosteroids are indicated in Pneumocystis pneumonia linked to human immunodeficiency virus when the room-air arterial oxygen pressure is below 70 mm Hg, and hers is 61. Prednisone is started as early as possible, ideally within 72 hours of starting treatment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.66 and a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 83% predicted. Which airflow grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her ratio of 0.66 confirms obstruction, and a post-bronchodilator 1-second volume of 83% predicted meets the grade 1 (mild) threshold of 80% or more. Her airflow is near normal even though the ratio is abnormal. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
